WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.265
Mental health funding gap.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.693
Can't treat what it offers to kill.

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:12.340
Canada spends approximately seven percent of health budgets on mental health compared to twelve percent or more in other OECD countries.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.329
Psychiatrist wait times exceed months in most provinces.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:20.261
Community mental health services are underfunded and fragmented.

00:00:20.261 --> 00:00:26.386
Meanwhile, the government proposed expanding MAID to individuals whose sole underlying condition is mental illness.

00:00:26.386 --> 00:00:34.511
The expansion was delayed three times because the healthcare system 'isn't ready' to provide adequate mental health services as an alternative to death.

00:00:34.511 --> 00:00:43.011
The delays themselves confirm the thesis: the system cannot treat mental illness adequately, so it delayed the expansion that would allow it to kill patients instead.

00:00:43.011 --> 00:00:49.193
When the treatment gap is finally closed by expanding MAID rather than expanding treatment, the gap becomes the pipeline.

00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:52.227
Three dimensions of the mental health funding gap.
